A||||5||ponya||mushy brakes||||5||
Z||000000||ponya||12-15-2000||09:46 PM||||I've posted this question before, but now I have changed everything. New calipers, rotors, brake hoses, master cylinder, etc. <BR>Basically its a new system. One problem, still do not have that I would trust in an emergency situation. My son insists its air in the system. Anyone know a guarenteed way to eliminate all the air. We've tried all the ways I know, maybe someone else has a better way. Any suggestions appreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||David Gamache||12-15-2000||10:55 PM||||I have had brake problems on a number of fieros and often find that the diverter valve, the gold colored metal block located in front of the master cylinder,often becomes internally contaminated and prevents the brake fluid from effectively reaching the calipers at the proper pressure.  If you have not replaced this piece I would recommend doing so.     ||||reg||10
Z||000002||PlasticWrapd||12-16-2000||04:07 AM||||Pressure bleeding often expels air that cannot be bled any other way.  In fact, many manufacturers recommend pressure bleeding for both the brakes and the clutch (Audi/VW is one of these).  Pressure bleeding is accomplished by a special adapter that replaces the cover to the master cylinder reservior.  Bleeder screws are opened and the fluid is forced, under pressure, through the hydraulic system.  Since the master cylinder isn't being used as the pump, any air bubbles that could get trapped in the secondary circuit are forced out.  If all else fails, give it a try.||||reg||3
Z||000003||strdream||12-16-2000||02:17 PM||||Did you have a panic stop at the onset of your brake trouble.<BR>An inbalance in the systems can cause the proportioning valve to "set" and divert your braking power to the other system.<BR>One way I check is with all four wheels of the ground have sone one pump up the brakes and hold the peddle down. now go around and try and turn each wheel by hand. If you can budge any wheel, than pump the brakes up HARD<BR>Some times that will reset the propotioning valve. now bleed again.<BR>Ross ||||reg||1
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||12-16-2000||10:50 PM||||I just bought the braided steel lines from the Fireo store. I have the same problem on my 88GT. I had new rotors, calipers and pads put on about 6 months ago. Then about two weeks ago, I had to do a sudden stop. Wow! I was pushing as hard as I could and my car slowed to a stop. No lock-up at all. Scared the hell out of me. I guess I should look at the diverter valve. Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-17-2000||01:56 PM||||This is a common problem with MC's that are mounted on an angle like Fiero has. I ran into an article recently, and posted I link to it here that delt with this. Of course now that I want it I can't find it. (I think it was on Motor Age www site.)<P>Esentially it said jack the back of the car nice an high durring bleeding. The tilt of the master cylinder can trap air in the very front that even power bleeding may not get out. Jacking the back of the car levels the MC to let the air out.  <P>Don't forget to drain off the MC tank before tilting the car or you will spill brake fluid all over things.<P>It's posible that the air bubble in the MC may even work itself out thru the tank if you park it nose down and very slowely pump the pedal, but most likely you need to fully bleed it with the nose down.||||reg||1
